WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.550
Our agent set a colleague a reminder for

2
00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:06.950
Saturday, for a lesson that doesn’t exist!

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.400
There’s no school on Saturday.

4
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.820
The timetable even said so.

5
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.020
It just carried on.

6
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.250
Today: when to trust an

7
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.370
agent, and how to check it.

8
00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.610
An agent sounds just as

9
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:24.630
sure when it’s wrong.

10
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.160
Darsi Tiny picks the right tool and every

11
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.700
detail about ninety-seven times in a

12
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.580
hundred, with wordings it learned.

13
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.290
With new wordings, it’s

14
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:36.390
closer to four in five.

15
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.620
And it never says, I’m not sure.

16
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.500
Three slips come up again and again.

17
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.900
It copies a name wrongly:

18
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.480
class 9Z became 9C.

19
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.520
It misreads a new way of asking.

20
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.700
And it carries on after a step fails,

21
00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.700
like that Saturday reminder.

22
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.430
So don’t judge an agent by

23
00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.650
how its answer sounds.

24
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.160
Check what it did.

25
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.000
Which tool did it call,

26
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.280
with which details?

27
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.170
What did the tool give back, and

28
00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.310
does the answer match it?

29
00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.330
Let’s check that Saturday reminder.

30
00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:16.310
The first call asked the

31
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.650
timetable for 6B on Saturday.

32
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.890
The result came back: no school.

33
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.610
Then it set a reminder

34
00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.870
that just says 6B: 6B.

35
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.560
The slip was there to see, in the result.

36
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.660
Think of a new teaching assistant.

37
00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.650
You’d let them fetch books on day one.

38
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.080
You’d check their register for weeks.

39
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.430
And you’d read any letter

40
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.750
home before it goes.

41
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:44.640
Quick check!

42
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.750
The agent’s answer sounds right.

43
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.800
What should you check, to

44
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:51.760
know it really is?

45
00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.550
The call and the result.

46
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.760
A confident sentence tells you nothing.

47
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.180
The call shows what it asked for, and

48
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.000
the result shows what came back.

49
00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.080
If the answer doesn’t match

50
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.720
the result, don’t use it.

51
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.600
Match your trust to the stakes.

52
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.690
Looking up the weather or a library

53
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.710
book: a quick check is enough.

54
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.640
Reminders and messages: you

55
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:19.480
approve them first.

56
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.390
Marks, attendance or anything about a

57
00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.590
child: a person decides, every time.

58
00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.250
In the app, an AI answer about

59
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.070
the Nile has four claims.

60
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.560
Mark each one: correct,

61
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.180
wrong, or check a source.

62
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.220
One is invented, yet sounds just as sure.

63
00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.000
Your turn.

64
00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.850
Which of these should an agent never do on

65
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.670
its own: look up tomorrow’s weather, find

66
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.470
a library book, or change a pupil’s mark?

67
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.360
Changing a mark: a person decides that.

68
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.070
Remember: sounding sure is not proof.

69
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.300
Check the call, the

70
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.980
details and the result.

71
00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.780
And match your trust to the stakes.

72
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.460
Try it in class tomorrow:

73
00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.340
play Spot the slip.

74
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.770
Write three short agent

75
00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.970
conversations on the board, each

76
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.930
with a call, a result and an answer.

77
00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.380
Hide a slip in two of them.

78
00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.610
Pupils find each slip, and rate

79
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.950
each job green, amber or red.
